    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. ive checked the utilization on core-rt-lyon-01 and it looks okay, not really hitting max capacity. but the jitters are still wild. i ran a longer ping test and some of the hops are just dropping packets entirely for a few seconds then coming back. i'm starting to wonder if there's some kind of layer 1 issue or if maybe something is causing intermittent interference on the line. do you think it could be a hardware fault or something like electromagnetic interference?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "If utilization on `core-rt-lyon-01` is fine, but you're seeing packet drops followed by recovery, that shifts the suspicion away from simple congestion and towards something more fundamental, like a physical layer issue. Intermittent packet loss without high utilization is often the signature of a bad physical link, which could be a faulty transceiver, bad cabling, or even electromagnetic interference (EMI). It's a bit like a nervous, flickering light bulb—it's not *always* broken, but when it is, it causes a major disruption. You might need to check the interface error counters (CRC errors, input/output drops) on the router interfaces connecting to those ISP hops.",
      "source": "target",
